WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.210
ANDREW TOY: All right.

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:07.200
Little bit loud.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.309
Hi, everyone.

00:00:08.309 --> 00:00:09.600
We're going to get started now.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.810
I'm aware that people are
still coming in from lunch,

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:14.520
but I think that's OK.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.470
My name is Andrew Toy.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:20.436
I am a product director within
the Android and Chrome team,

00:00:20.436 --> 00:00:21.810
and I'm here to
talk to you today

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:25.500
about how we see
devices and the cloud

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.440
coming together to help
build the connected workspace

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:29.280
of the future.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.280
How we see the devices
adding to the cloud,

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.160
and the cloud capabilities
being additive to our devices

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:36.330
strategy.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:40.970
Obviously, with Google we have
Android and we have Chrome OS.

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:43.410
So those are our platforms,
and we definitely

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.810
build those to be cloud
oriented, but most

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:48.180
of what I talk about
today is basically a way

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.600
that you can look at a general
devices strategy plugging back

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.850
into the cloud.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:56.860
So many of you will
already know this.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:59.650
These are statistics that
we all see flying by,

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:02.350
but there's a lot of
confusion and there's

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:06.370
a lot of progress going around
how we use devices today

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:07.940
in the workplace.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:10.570
Many, many people,
everyone here I'm sure,

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:14.440
are using their devices
everyday for work,

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:17.050
but what does that really mean
in the context of extending

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:18.570
the workplace?

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:21.730
And we all know that IoT,
the Internet of Things,

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:24.310
a very heavily overused word,
but the Internet of Things

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:25.090
is coming.

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:28.960
Those are also devices, and how
do those plug into the cloud

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:32.290
and plug into a general
mobility strategy.

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:33.970
So the way that we
see it at Google

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:37.300
is that while we've all talked
about mobile for a while,

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:40.330
mobility really is more
about the next generation

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:42.490
of modern end point,
and the next generation

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:44.830
of modern device
that is connecting

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:48.454
to our centralized data
systems within a company.

00:01:48.454 --> 00:01:49.870
Now that could be
in a data center

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:51.160
and that could be in a cloud.

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:54.400
We think that that's moving all
towards the cloud, obviously,

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:56.080
but it doesn't have
to be the cloud.

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:59.290
But we definitely see device
proliferation increasing

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:01.030
the amount of data
that is actually

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:04.000
going into all of our
systems, the amount

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.760
of data that could
be available to us

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:09.094
as people who operate
within companies,

00:02:09.094 --> 00:02:11.260
increasing the amount of
data that's available to us

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:13.200
as application developers.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.490
And bringing all
of this together

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:18.130
is really where
we see the future

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:20.706
of application development
in a mobile first world.

00:02:20.706 --> 00:02:22.330
And the next generation
of that is just

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:26.140
being an internet
everywhere world.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:28.040
So the way I like
to think about this,

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:29.710
and this is a
slightly different way

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:31.540
of looking at the
mobile problem,

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:34.180
a slightly different way of
looking at the devices problem,

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:38.000
is that really what
we all want is data.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:41.350
Data is what is valuable
to all of our businesses.

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:44.770
Data is what is valuable
to application developers.

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:48.280
And the reason we do all
of this stuff around mobile

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:51.130
is actually to proliferate data.

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:54.070
To sort of it's available
to us all the time,

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:55.780
and then we feel like
we can take action

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:59.500
upon that data in any
place, at any given moment.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:01.930
So that's the traditional
knowledge worker definition,

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:03.900
where you have say your email.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.880
And one of the very first
use cases for mobility

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:10.600
was extending email out so we
could have email everywhere.

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:12.880
Because email is that
most generic way that we

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:14.440
can deliver information, right?

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:17.220
So if you get a sales report,
it can be emailed to you.

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:20.230
If you approve say
a expense, that

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:22.044
could be done,
theoretically, over email.

00:03:22.044 --> 00:03:23.710
So what made email
special was that it's

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:25.840
a very general
transport that allows

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:28.000
lots of different kinds
of data to be sent

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:31.090
to us as individuals, and that
was very enticing and very

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:32.260
appealing.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:34.840
But the extension
of this is that we

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:38.320
want all businesses to become
data centric businesses.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:41.560
For business decisions to
be made around the data,

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:44.020
and to make it be
generally more available.

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:46.660
We hear from a lot of customers
that this is absolutely

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:49.750
what they want, and we
see devices and mobility

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:54.330
being as essential in
actually achieving this goal.

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:56.430
But let's take it
up another level

00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:59.170
and say well, why
do we want data?

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:02.430
We don't want data for the sake
of just having stuff, right?

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:05.100
More stuff is not what we
actually want in our day

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:06.030
to day lives.

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:09.480
But what we believe here at
Google is that you cannot

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:12.000
improve what you cannot measure.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.790
So measurement is actually
really, really important.

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:17.441
And it's obvious, and we
all know this at some level.

00:04:17.441 --> 00:04:18.899
But it's something
that's very easy

00:04:18.899 --> 00:04:22.019
to lose sight of unless you
really bake it into your day

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:22.980
to day thinking.

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:24.510
And for us, it's
baked into the way

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:27.720
we think about our
entire mobility strategy.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:31.290
So the first stage is,
how do we measure things

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:32.700
within our environment?

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:34.520
We can be measuring
productivity,

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:36.660
we can be measuring individual
output of employees.

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:38.160
And that's one part of it.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:40.950
But we could be measuring
spaces, measuring

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:44.870
how spaces are used, measuring
collaboration, measuring

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:47.010
manufacturing within a factory.

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:49.560
We want to make sure that we
have insights and actually can

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:52.020
gather information
in all the places

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:54.600
that it is available to us.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:57.450
So an example that
I'd like to give

00:04:57.450 --> 00:05:01.530
is thinking about getting
access to an experience

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:03.380
that many of us have.

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:06.744
An experience with say you
go to a fast food restaurant.

00:05:06.744 --> 00:05:08.160
I go to fast food
restaurants more

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:10.020
that I generally
like to admit, but I

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:11.850
go to fast food restaurants.

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:13.110
So you go there.

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:15.710
A lot of fast food
restaurants here in the US

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:18.930
will actually know
how much they actually

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:22.770
sell to the people who
come to their stores.

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:25.050
It's something that
they will know, right?

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:28.470
So they know that they sell
x number of hamburgers,

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:29.935
or x number of
tacos, or whatever

00:05:29.935 --> 00:05:31.060
it is that they're selling.

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:34.250
And they'll know that because
they have their point of sale

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:35.600
in their store.

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:36.770
So they have data.

00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:38.360
And they can optimize
on that data,

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:39.890
and they do, and they
can make projections,

00:05:39.890 --> 00:05:41.890
they can deal with supply
chain, and they can do

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:43.290
all those things on that data.

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:45.770
But many of these
stores actually

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:48.950
do not know how many people
come into their store.

00:05:48.950 --> 00:05:51.212
All they know is
how much they sell.

00:05:51.212 --> 00:05:53.420
That's all they know, how
much they sell because it's

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:54.604
in the point of sale.

00:05:54.604 --> 00:05:57.020
They don't actually know how
many people come in the door.

00:05:57.020 --> 00:06:00.050
So if you ask what the
conversion rate of is

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:01.700
for someone coming
in the door, it's

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:03.466
actually very hard
to tell because you

00:06:03.466 --> 00:06:04.590
don't know what's going on.

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:07.727
You don't know when you
sold one taco whether or not

00:06:07.727 --> 00:06:09.560
that one taco was sold
to one individual who

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:13.190
came in, or did a family of
six come in and order one taco.

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:16.440
That information is invisible
to the actual company.

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:20.790
So instrumentation is actually
a slightly less obvious thing.

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:23.330
It's obvious you want to
instrument your sales,

00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:25.890
but it might be slightly
less obvious to instrument.

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:28.400
Well how many people are
coming into the store?

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:31.150
How many people are
parking in my parking lot,

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:33.180
and do they all
come into my store?

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:34.860
How many, when they
come into my store,

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:37.497
are they all ordering and then
taking it back out to the car,

00:06:37.497 --> 00:06:39.830
or are they ordering it and
then sitting down at a table

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:41.030
and eating it there?

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:45.020
These are all actually important
operational fields for someone

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:48.020
to run their restaurant,
but a lot of businesses

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:50.462
are blind to this data today.

00:06:50.462 --> 00:06:51.920
So I deliberately
give that example

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:54.300
because it's probably an
experience we all have,

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:56.740
but not something we're
thinking about every day.

00:06:56.740 --> 00:06:58.550
And instrumentation,
and this word

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:01.580
we have of just gathering this
information of all the data

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:05.380
that's important to your
business is the first step.

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:07.540
The second step is
analyzing that data.

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:11.070
So first of all, we get the data
and then we analyze the data.

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:12.490
So the analysis stages.

00:07:12.490 --> 00:07:14.490
First of all, you need
to put the data somewhere

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:18.390
which is useful and common, and
you need to put in clean data.

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:21.330
And second of all, you then
need to sort of pull out

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:22.350
insights from that.

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:24.600
We with Google are
very good at this.

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:26.160
Google Cloud is
very good at this,

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:27.960
and we have many tools,
ranging all the way

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:30.565
from just query tools like
BigQuery to store the data

00:07:30.565 --> 00:07:33.840
and pull stuff out, over to
machine learning, that can

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:35.170
actually help optimize fields.

00:07:35.170 --> 00:07:37.740
And I'm sure many of you have
heard about those capabilities,

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:40.620
but that's the phase that
we're talking about, analysis.

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:42.115
And finally,
there's improvement.

00:07:42.115 --> 00:07:43.740
Which is we don't do
all this unless we

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:44.790
want to improve, right?

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:45.840
Business outcomes.

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:47.340
We want to increase
revenue, we want

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:48.680
to increase the
number of people who

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:50.221
come to a store,
the number of people

00:07:50.221 --> 00:07:53.196
who order the actual products in
the store, or buy in the store.

00:07:53.196 --> 00:07:54.320
And that's the improvement.

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:56.070
And then you go and do it again.

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:58.740
You don't go through a cycle
where you just say well,

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:00.192
we improved it, let's move on.

00:08:00.192 --> 00:08:02.400
You improve it, you measure
it again, you improve it,

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:03.540
you measure it again.

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:07.440
Very simple concepts, but
fundamental to how we can

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:08.910
actually do this better now.

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:11.340
Much, much better
with a combination

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:13.990
of devices and the cloud.

00:08:13.990 --> 00:08:19.500
So the idea is that by
using our fleet of devices,

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:22.290
we can actually put very cost
effective instrumentation

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:23.759
within any number of workplaces.

00:08:23.759 --> 00:08:25.800
If you want to know how
your conference rooms are

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:29.784
being used within your offices,
that can be easily measured.

00:08:29.784 --> 00:08:31.200
If you want to
know, that examples

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:33.260
I gave before, how a
restaurant is operating,

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:34.500
that can be measured.

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:37.500
If you want to know how
a hospital is operating

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:39.179
and how the patients
are interacting,

00:08:39.179 --> 00:08:40.289
that can be measured.

00:08:40.289 --> 00:08:43.200
Many, many things that were kind
of difficult to measure before,

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:45.540
or were measured
ad hoc, are now be

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:48.090
able to be measured
in a much cleaner way.

00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:50.160
And that data, it can
actually stream up and be

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:51.840
placed in a central place.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:56.160
So if you imagine
for a moment that you

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:58.050
operate many, many
restaurants across the US.

00:08:58.050 --> 00:08:59.970
I'll just stick with this
example, quick service.

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:01.386
And you have all
your franchisees,

00:09:01.386 --> 00:09:03.810
you have all this data,
imagine that you suddenly had

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:05.250
access to all this rich data.

00:09:05.250 --> 00:09:07.110
Like the number of people
coming in, the number of people

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:08.490
going through the drive
through, the number of people

00:09:08.490 --> 00:09:09.900
using your parking lot,
the number of people

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:11.940
ordering the products,
the number of people

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:12.960
using the tables.

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:16.440
Like how every employee is
actually performing on a day

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:18.930
to day basis, how many
things that they're making.

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:21.460
All of this data
has to go somewhere.

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:25.380
And that place is actually going
to be in the cloud, definitely.

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:27.014
It doesn't make
sense for businesses

00:09:27.014 --> 00:09:28.680
to build out all their
own data systems,

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:31.290
absorb all this new data
that they now have access to.

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:33.330
The cloud is where
it's going to go.

00:09:33.330 --> 00:09:36.219
And as Google, obviously
we have every component

00:09:36.219 --> 00:09:37.260
of this particular story.

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:39.640
We have great devices
between Android and Chrome

00:09:39.640 --> 00:09:42.480
and our Internet of
Things sensors platforms.

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:44.580
We have the GCP, the
Google Cloud Platform

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:47.100
to absorb all that data
and provide analysis.

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:49.620
We have layers on top of it
to provide productivity tools,

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:52.100
and general analysis
tools in G Suite,

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:54.210
and a lot of different
applications,

00:09:54.210 --> 00:09:57.510
just cloud applications
across the portfolio.

00:09:57.510 --> 00:09:58.980
Like Google Maps, for example.

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:00.600
That could be used to
garnish the data that's

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:01.266
being pulled in.

00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:07.410
So the place that we see this
going, from a connected work

00:10:07.410 --> 00:10:10.680
spaces portfolio standpoint,
is that first of all, we

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:14.130
want to make sure everything
is going to be connected.

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:15.820
Everything is going
to be connected.

00:10:15.820 --> 00:10:20.100
So every device, every
mobile platform, every sensor

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:22.290
should be thought
holistically and together.

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:24.990
You should have one thing for
running your email, saying well

00:10:24.990 --> 00:10:26.970
how am I going to
do BYOD with email?

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:27.470
This way.

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:29.594
Then how am I going to do
my corporate liable fleet

00:10:29.594 --> 00:10:30.710
for document management?

00:10:30.710 --> 00:10:31.570
OK, this way.

00:10:31.570 --> 00:10:33.810
Then how am I going to
do IoT smart sensors?

00:10:33.810 --> 00:10:34.900
This way.

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:37.050
These are all
actually end points,

00:10:37.050 --> 00:10:41.404
and all devices are going to
be connected to the internet.

00:10:41.404 --> 00:10:43.320
You can add the security
stuff on top of that,

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:44.778
and I'll talk about
it in a moment,

00:10:44.778 --> 00:10:47.190
but they're all going
to be connected devices.

00:10:47.190 --> 00:10:49.410
Maybe not tomorrow,
but definitely

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:52.650
within three to five years,
everything we will all be doing

00:10:52.650 --> 00:10:53.454
will be connected.

00:10:53.454 --> 00:10:54.870
And so the idea
we have is we want

00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:57.390
to lay the foundations
right now of our strategies

00:10:57.390 --> 00:11:00.840
to be able to accommodate that
future massive proliferation

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:02.280
of devices.

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:04.020
You want everything to be smart.

00:11:04.020 --> 00:11:07.320
And when we say smart, that
means that data centric point

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:07.830
of view.

00:11:07.830 --> 00:11:09.990
So think about where your
data is being gathered.

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:13.080
Think about how to secure that
data, how to move it around.

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:15.780
Where you're going to build your
repositories and your caches.

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:18.570
Are you going to actually
gather that data, for example,

00:11:18.570 --> 00:11:20.670
at the individual
store level and then

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:21.810
move it up to the cloud?

00:11:21.810 --> 00:11:23.226
Are you going to
have every device

00:11:23.226 --> 00:11:25.380
stream that data straight
into your central data

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:29.040
centers within the cloud so that
you can actually individually

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:30.602
have those use APIs?

00:11:30.602 --> 00:11:32.310
All those decisions
are the ones you want

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:36.390
to be thinking about right now.

00:11:36.390 --> 00:11:38.800
And we'll talk about
security in a moment.

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:42.849
So this is the sort of
easy way to break down

00:11:42.849 --> 00:11:44.640
the phases when we're
talking to customers,

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:46.723
and the way we're thinking
about it her as Google.

00:11:46.723 --> 00:11:50.160
The first stage is think
about how to better instrument

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:52.620
your business.

00:11:52.620 --> 00:11:56.300
Giving your employees devices to
get their email have documents

00:11:56.300 --> 00:11:58.749
was the first stage
of instrumentation.

00:11:58.749 --> 00:12:00.540
That's what all our
companies were actually

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:02.520
doing when we actually
give out those devices

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:04.800
that like interact with work
and interactive work data

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:05.980
work applications.

00:12:05.980 --> 00:12:08.834
We are instrumenting
the employees.

00:12:08.834 --> 00:12:10.500
The next thing is you
want to instrument

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:13.680
probably, [INAUDIBLE] on the
bottom, the spaces, right?

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:17.020
So areas like this
room, for example.

00:12:17.020 --> 00:12:18.880
So we know how many
people are in the room,

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:21.088
we know which of the people
in this room are Googlers

00:12:21.088 --> 00:12:23.250
and which people
are from outside.

00:12:23.250 --> 00:12:25.860
Instrumenting your spaces
could be from retail spaces

00:12:25.860 --> 00:12:27.810
to hospitals, anywhere
with brick and mortar,

00:12:27.810 --> 00:12:30.930
conferences, conference
room space, micro kitchens.

00:12:30.930 --> 00:12:34.260
All those kinds of things
is where IoT comes in.

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:36.000
And that's where
a lot of that data

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:38.730
is going to be coming in
over the next few years.

00:12:38.730 --> 00:12:40.890
Another area that people
don't often think about

00:12:40.890 --> 00:12:43.830
is instrumenting the
customers as well.

00:12:43.830 --> 00:12:45.540
How are you going
to actually get data

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:47.010
from your customer base?

00:12:47.010 --> 00:12:49.230
And BYOD, and
applications, and mobility

00:12:49.230 --> 00:12:51.360
can actually feature
into this, as well as

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:54.740
plugging into the devices they
have within their environments.

00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:58.315
So you know when Amazon
gives out devices

00:12:58.315 --> 00:13:01.650
so that you can order
from Amazon at home,

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:05.954
that is a way you can
instrument your customer base.

00:13:05.954 --> 00:13:07.620
And all of these data
streams should not

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:09.610
be thought of as separate.

00:13:09.610 --> 00:13:11.760
And that's what we have
all the little interweaving

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:13.537
of the actual streams there.

00:13:13.537 --> 00:13:15.120
All of this is going
to come together.

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:19.050
And you want to think about how
you actually join and analyze

00:13:19.050 --> 00:13:22.440
all this information in a way
where you can easily gather it

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:26.510
with the instrumentation phase,
and then run queries upon it,

00:13:26.510 --> 00:13:29.460
and gain insights in
that red analysis phase.

00:13:29.460 --> 00:13:31.380
And that's really where
the cloud comes in

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:33.990
because it's all too
easy for individual silos

00:13:33.990 --> 00:13:36.120
and individual groups
within the enterprise

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:37.410
to be thinking about this.

00:13:37.410 --> 00:13:40.300
And you end up storing all
your data at individual silos,

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:42.300
and really, that's just
not what you want to do.

00:13:42.300 --> 00:13:44.115
To get real insights,
you want to be

00:13:44.115 --> 00:13:47.130
able to join this data to pull
out really cool information.

00:13:47.130 --> 00:13:49.530
And that's where machine
learning comes in as well.

00:13:49.530 --> 00:13:51.240
The more siloed data
there is, the harder

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:52.950
it is for you to
eventually use something

00:13:52.950 --> 00:13:55.350
like ML to pull out insights.

00:13:55.350 --> 00:13:57.780
And to be future proof,
you want to be thinking

00:13:57.780 --> 00:14:00.731
about your data warehousing
strategy, your device strategy

00:14:00.731 --> 00:14:01.230
right now.

00:14:04.370 --> 00:14:07.550
So connected devices, and
we talked about this before,

00:14:07.550 --> 00:14:11.010
are absolutely going
to just be everywhere.

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:13.430
That's just the world
we're going to live in.

00:14:13.430 --> 00:14:16.580
And you know, I'm a mobile guy.

00:14:16.580 --> 00:14:18.607
I've been in mobile
for a long time.

00:14:18.607 --> 00:14:20.690
I've been mobile all the
way back to the days when

00:14:20.690 --> 00:14:23.840
we're using WAP on phones,
in case anyone ever did

00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:25.280
WAP programming.

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:28.670
We all carry the certain
scars that you carry if you're

00:14:28.670 --> 00:14:30.290
coming from those days.

00:14:30.290 --> 00:14:32.240
But I've been in mobile
for a very long time,

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:33.770
and mobile was
defined for a while

00:14:33.770 --> 00:14:35.870
to be like, well first
of all telephony.

00:14:35.870 --> 00:14:38.630
Then it was telephony with
tiny, tiny little bits of data

00:14:38.630 --> 00:14:41.580
using something like WAP
in carrier walled gardens.

00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:44.300
Then it was, OK
now mobile is OK,

00:14:44.300 --> 00:14:46.250
we're going to just
start to put the web

00:14:46.250 --> 00:14:48.830
and put the internet
on our devices.

00:14:48.830 --> 00:14:50.752
Then the iPhone came
and said well no,

00:14:50.752 --> 00:14:52.460
these are tiny little
computers, and then

00:14:52.460 --> 00:14:55.781
mobile really means how do I
develop apps for this new place

00:14:55.781 --> 00:14:57.530
because we were just
put to go to all web,

00:14:57.530 --> 00:15:01.050
and now we have to develop apps
for Android, and for iPhones,

00:15:01.050 --> 00:15:03.590
and things like that, and
put them in the app stores.

00:15:03.590 --> 00:15:08.060
Mobile now, I think actually
just means everything.

00:15:08.060 --> 00:15:10.010
Everything is going
to be a mobile device.

00:15:10.010 --> 00:15:12.350
Don't even think about as a
separate category anymore.

00:15:12.350 --> 00:15:15.920
Just think about your devices
and endpoint strategy.

00:15:15.920 --> 00:15:18.140
Don't think about what's
my mobile strategy,

00:15:18.140 --> 00:15:20.390
and what's my other
strategy, right?

00:15:20.390 --> 00:15:23.840
I used to say this, and I think
we're actually right here now,

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:26.570
but when I gave a talk maybe
two, three years ago, what

00:15:26.570 --> 00:15:29.860
I used to say was
I know it feels

00:15:29.860 --> 00:15:31.630
like mobile is its own thing.

00:15:31.630 --> 00:15:33.300
I know you feel
like you're going

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:35.770
to have your own mobile group,
your own mobile application

00:15:35.770 --> 00:15:38.230
group, you're going to
hire mobile developers,

00:15:38.230 --> 00:15:40.370
it feel like its own
separate category.

00:15:40.370 --> 00:15:42.850
But trust me, mobile,
a few years ago,

00:15:42.850 --> 00:15:46.090
mobile is just going to
be like the term internet.

00:15:46.090 --> 00:15:49.330
So in the late 90s, the exact
same thing was happening.

00:15:49.330 --> 00:15:52.060
You had your internet group,
you had your internet experts,

00:15:52.060 --> 00:15:53.496
you hired internet programmers.

00:15:53.496 --> 00:15:55.870
And it was like wow, there's
this whole internet category

00:15:55.870 --> 00:15:58.090
that a business
would have and say we

00:15:58.090 --> 00:15:59.950
need to have an internet group.

00:15:59.950 --> 00:16:03.460
And now very, very few companies
have an internet group anymore.

00:16:03.460 --> 00:16:05.950
The internet is actually
just built all the way

00:16:05.950 --> 00:16:07.450
through the culture
of the company.

00:16:07.450 --> 00:16:09.520
It's built all the way
through every strategy

00:16:09.520 --> 00:16:10.270
the company makes.

00:16:10.270 --> 00:16:11.537
You don't just say well, yeah.

00:16:11.537 --> 00:16:13.870
How do we deal with, the
internet guys will handle that.

00:16:13.870 --> 00:16:15.400
It just sounds outmoded.

00:16:15.400 --> 00:16:19.090
That's where we immanently
are with mobile right now.

00:16:19.090 --> 00:16:20.680
Again, it's we're
just on the cusp.

00:16:20.680 --> 00:16:22.390
You might intuitively
feel that way,

00:16:22.390 --> 00:16:24.400
but you will feel
that way very soon.

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:25.900
So I encourage
everyone in this room

00:16:25.900 --> 00:16:28.108
to stop thinking about mobile
being a separate thing,

00:16:28.108 --> 00:16:31.930
mobile is now fundamental and
as existential to every process

00:16:31.930 --> 00:16:32.700
that you do.

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:36.027
It is existential to every
business's ability to compete.

00:16:36.027 --> 00:16:38.110
When you think about
something to be mobile first,

00:16:38.110 --> 00:16:40.570
even that is a phrase that
we're kind of moving away from.

00:16:40.570 --> 00:16:42.800
Mobile just needs to
be in the conversation.

00:16:42.800 --> 00:16:45.070
It's just like an endpoint
discussion, a device

00:16:45.070 --> 00:16:45.940
discussion.

00:16:45.940 --> 00:16:50.230
And we feel we were seeing this
as well on the devices side.

00:16:50.230 --> 00:16:52.030
So when people
come and say well,

00:16:52.030 --> 00:16:54.370
how are we going to
build a mobile app?

00:16:54.370 --> 00:16:57.250
The first thing I say is, well,
what do you mean by mobile?

00:16:57.250 --> 00:16:58.570
What exactly is mobile?

00:16:58.570 --> 00:17:01.240
Where does mobile end and
where does laptop begin,

00:17:01.240 --> 00:17:04.420
or convertible begin, or
tablet begin, or phablet begin?

00:17:04.420 --> 00:17:06.770
Or are you saying that
tablet is part of mobile?

00:17:06.770 --> 00:17:09.187
And there's actually not a
consistent definition for that.

00:17:09.187 --> 00:17:10.603
Sometimes it's
like, oh well, it's

00:17:10.603 --> 00:17:12.380
based on screen size
and all these things.

00:17:12.380 --> 00:17:14.130
But really, that doesn't
really make sense

00:17:14.130 --> 00:17:16.780
because maybe if you have
a great application written

00:17:16.780 --> 00:17:19.390
for your phone, and you
want to blow it up so

00:17:19.390 --> 00:17:20.910
it runs on this
screen right here.

00:17:20.910 --> 00:17:22.900
Does that mean it's
not mobile anymore?

00:17:22.900 --> 00:17:24.790
Even though it's an
Android application.

00:17:24.790 --> 00:17:26.470
That doesn't really
make sense, right?

00:17:26.470 --> 00:17:28.136
So what we're seeing
at Google, and this

00:17:28.136 --> 00:17:30.640
is from our Chrome OS and
our Android strategies,

00:17:30.640 --> 00:17:34.330
is that we really just think now
about capabilities of devices.

00:17:34.330 --> 00:17:36.400
This is how you want
to use this device,

00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:38.860
and how appropriate is this
device for this particular use

00:17:38.860 --> 00:17:40.500
case?

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:42.370
And when you think
about it that way,

00:17:42.370 --> 00:17:44.237
then everything
becomes a continuum.

00:17:44.237 --> 00:17:45.820
You shouldn't have
a separate security

00:17:45.820 --> 00:17:48.140
story for your small
devices, your IP devices,

00:17:48.140 --> 00:17:51.340
and have a simple security
story for your signage

00:17:51.340 --> 00:17:53.980
or super secure use story for
your actual knowledge worker

00:17:53.980 --> 00:17:54.797
laptops.

00:17:54.797 --> 00:17:56.630
Everything should be
on the same principles.

00:17:56.630 --> 00:17:58.870
And it's just certain
properties of the device.

00:17:58.870 --> 00:18:02.380
Like whether it has its own
separate soldier connection,

00:18:02.380 --> 00:18:06.040
whether or not it needs
to run local applications,

00:18:06.040 --> 00:18:07.660
or does it just run
web applications?

00:18:07.660 --> 00:18:10.450
These are just orthogonal
things you decide as you come up

00:18:10.450 --> 00:18:11.700
with your mobile strategy.

00:18:14.420 --> 00:18:17.980
So this is a good
example of that.

00:18:17.980 --> 00:18:20.990
People might notice already,
but about a year ago,

00:18:20.990 --> 00:18:22.990
I think, maybe a little
less, we announced

00:18:22.990 --> 00:18:25.440
that Android applications
were coming to Chrome OS.

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:27.640
So previously on Chrome
OS, you could only

00:18:27.640 --> 00:18:31.000
run things through Chrome like
the web, HTML5 applications.

00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:32.170
Makes absolute sense, right?

00:18:32.170 --> 00:18:33.940
It's called Chrome OS.

00:18:33.940 --> 00:18:36.830
But now what we decided to do
was say well, you know what?

00:18:36.830 --> 00:18:39.640
Sometimes it makes for you to
have some local information,

00:18:39.640 --> 00:18:41.050
to have some local data.

00:18:41.050 --> 00:18:43.290
So we're going to take
the secure paradigm

00:18:43.290 --> 00:18:45.190
we have on Chrome OS.

00:18:45.190 --> 00:18:46.810
We're going to add
to it the ability

00:18:46.810 --> 00:18:48.850
to deploy applications
via Google Play,

00:18:48.850 --> 00:18:52.130
and let any Android
application run that device.

00:18:52.130 --> 00:18:54.500
And what that means is that
as Chrome OS moves and says,

00:18:54.500 --> 00:18:56.125
well, it's not in a
laptop form factor,

00:18:56.125 --> 00:18:58.000
but now it's in a
convertible form factor.

00:18:58.000 --> 00:19:00.580
You can take off that screen
from the Chrome OS device,

00:19:00.580 --> 00:19:03.850
run it as a tablet, and have
full access to all the Android

00:19:03.850 --> 00:19:05.440
applications in
the Play Store that

00:19:05.440 --> 00:19:09.340
are optimized for tablet and for
mobile and just have them run.

00:19:09.340 --> 00:19:11.440
Because now that
Chrome OS device

00:19:11.440 --> 00:19:13.870
is literally an Android device.

00:19:13.870 --> 00:19:15.730
It's not emulated,
it's not pixel pushed,

00:19:15.730 --> 00:19:16.960
it's not simulated.

00:19:16.960 --> 00:19:19.660
Chrome OS devices
are Android devices,

00:19:19.660 --> 00:19:22.960
even when they come in large
17 inch laptop form factors.

00:19:22.960 --> 00:19:25.946
So that's what I mean by that
blurring where at some point,

00:19:25.946 --> 00:19:27.820
rather than trying to
reconcile this model of

00:19:27.820 --> 00:19:29.740
is that a mobile
device, or should I

00:19:29.740 --> 00:19:32.290
use my mobile security story
for that, or my laptop security

00:19:32.290 --> 00:19:32.860
story?

00:19:32.860 --> 00:19:34.516
It's actually better
just to revamp

00:19:34.516 --> 00:19:36.640
the way you think about
your endpoints and that way

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:38.800
you think about your
development to actually just

00:19:38.800 --> 00:19:41.410
encompass and embrace
this choice that we have,

00:19:41.410 --> 00:19:43.142
versus forcing yourself
into like knots.

00:19:43.142 --> 00:19:44.350
Trying to pigeon hole things.

00:19:44.350 --> 00:19:47.622
Which is what I see a
lot of people doing.

00:19:47.622 --> 00:19:49.080
And we're trying
to help with this.

00:19:49.080 --> 00:19:50.830
We're trying to help
with this by bringing

00:19:50.830 --> 00:19:52.500
this modern way of
doing application

00:19:52.500 --> 00:19:56.600
management, this modern
way of doing security,

00:19:56.600 --> 00:20:00.210
and building it into all of our
device endpoints by default.

00:20:00.210 --> 00:20:02.790
You'll see this in Android,
you'll see this it Chrome.

00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:05.180
So everything we have
here is available on both

00:20:05.180 --> 00:20:06.000
those platforms.

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:09.014
Getting comment management
APIs so you can always

00:20:09.014 --> 00:20:10.680
feel like you're in
control of your data

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:12.260
and in control of
your endpoints.

00:20:12.260 --> 00:20:15.450
And bringing those across all
of the endpoints we work on.

00:20:15.450 --> 00:20:17.580
And we'll always
prioritize that.

00:20:17.580 --> 00:20:19.192
And like I said,
you can actually,

00:20:19.192 --> 00:20:21.650
many of the people using other
end points, Windows devices,

00:20:21.650 --> 00:20:23.970
Mac OS devices, you'll
see that same thing

00:20:23.970 --> 00:20:25.320
happening across the board.

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:27.070
But we at Google
definitely believe in it.

00:20:29.800 --> 00:20:32.970
And of course, we see
cloud native people.

00:20:32.970 --> 00:20:36.300
Cloud native application
usage requires the cloud,

00:20:36.300 --> 00:20:38.820
and so the security story
and the application of story

00:20:38.820 --> 00:20:41.280
extends across, not
just the endpoints,

00:20:41.280 --> 00:20:43.422
but all the way back
into the cloud as well.

00:20:43.422 --> 00:20:44.880
So when we say
well, how do we make

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:48.450
sure that the applications you
develop for mobile or now run

00:20:48.450 --> 00:20:50.340
on all of these
different devices,

00:20:50.340 --> 00:20:53.072
the cloud is an integral
part of that story.

00:20:53.072 --> 00:20:54.530
We're no longer
living in the world

00:20:54.530 --> 00:20:56.530
where you're going to do
virus scanning locally,

00:20:56.530 --> 00:20:59.550
and then have local firewalls,
and have local malware

00:20:59.550 --> 00:21:01.230
detection, all these things.

00:21:01.230 --> 00:21:04.680
You're going to have modern
endpoints with strict security

00:21:04.680 --> 00:21:07.290
policies, manageable
by it from the cloud,

00:21:07.290 --> 00:21:10.860
and the cloud will actually add
to the security capabilities

00:21:10.860 --> 00:21:12.430
of those endpoints as well.

00:21:12.430 --> 00:21:14.400
And so the two
will work together

00:21:14.400 --> 00:21:16.942
because we're going towards
this always connected world.

00:21:16.942 --> 00:21:18.900
And so yes, we'll always
think about how things

00:21:18.900 --> 00:21:21.240
work in an offline
scenario, but we

00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:22.770
want to optimize
it, take advantage

00:21:22.770 --> 00:21:25.101
of the incredible
power we get by having

00:21:25.101 --> 00:21:26.975
the cloud available to
endpoints and devices.

00:21:30.450 --> 00:21:33.120
So we think about this
as a general solution,

00:21:33.120 --> 00:21:34.200
a general platform.

00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:36.030
At Google we think very broad.

00:21:36.030 --> 00:21:38.440
But we see the applicability
of this approach

00:21:38.440 --> 00:21:40.635
into individual
verticals as well.

00:21:40.635 --> 00:21:41.760
And we've listed some here.

00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:43.770
And if folks are in
from specific verticals,

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:46.260
please do ask questions, or
come up at the end and we

