00:24:10.140 --> 00:24:12.740
And for the past 13,
14 years my focus

00:24:12.740 --> 00:24:14.870
has been in enterprise mobility.

00:24:14.870 --> 00:24:17.240
And enterprise mobility,
what we really mean by that,

00:24:17.240 --> 00:24:21.920
is how do we help our
workers, our employees

00:24:21.920 --> 00:24:22.972
with mobile solutions?

00:24:22.972 --> 00:24:24.680
How do we make them
more productive, more

00:24:24.680 --> 00:24:26.690
effective in the
work that they do,

00:24:26.690 --> 00:24:29.540
and how do we provide them
with new technology to do that?

00:24:29.540 --> 00:24:32.660
And I think right now we're at a
really, really interesting time

00:24:32.660 --> 00:24:35.570
because we have a lot
of innovation to bring.

00:24:35.570 --> 00:24:37.295
And to me, as a
technologist, as someone

00:24:37.295 --> 00:24:39.170
who's really passionate
about all this stuff,

00:24:39.170 --> 00:24:41.030
it's a super exciting time.

00:24:41.030 --> 00:24:43.400
And we're doing some really
cool things with our clients

00:24:43.400 --> 00:24:47.030
that are really changing
the way that they do work.

00:24:47.030 --> 00:24:49.340
So I wanted to maybe
just spend a little bit

00:24:49.340 --> 00:24:54.590
of time on where we are when it
comes to enterprise mobility.

00:24:54.590 --> 00:24:58.310
And I actually say we're in
our third phase of mobility.

00:24:58.310 --> 00:25:01.460
And what I mean by that is,
when we first started out,

00:25:01.460 --> 00:25:05.190
when we were talking
about mobility 1.0,

00:25:05.190 --> 00:25:08.580
this was really just getting
devices out to our workers,

00:25:08.580 --> 00:25:10.460
and they were pretty
simple devices.

00:25:10.460 --> 00:25:13.040
It was typically a corporate
liable environment.

00:25:13.040 --> 00:25:14.800
We're giving them the devices.

00:25:14.800 --> 00:25:16.430
And you really,
all you were doing

00:25:16.430 --> 00:25:19.700
was email, calendar, very
basic stuff like that, right?

00:25:19.700 --> 00:25:22.570
Maybe those were the
old BlackBerry days

00:25:22.570 --> 00:25:25.910
when we were doing
that kind of stuff.

00:25:25.910 --> 00:25:28.420
Then we shifted to
mobility 2.0, which

00:25:28.420 --> 00:25:32.090
is really more about
transactional applications.

00:25:32.090 --> 00:25:36.080
Now we're starting to do more
simple things with our devices.

00:25:36.080 --> 00:25:38.570
Not just email, but now
we're actually maybe

00:25:38.570 --> 00:25:41.000
we're doing some
dashboarding, or we're

00:25:41.000 --> 00:25:45.020
getting some basic time and
expense type of applications,

00:25:45.020 --> 00:25:47.780
or we're able to
order things, or we're

00:25:47.780 --> 00:25:50.690
able to just do
simple transactions.

00:25:50.690 --> 00:25:54.020
Things that we used to do
on our laptops or desktops

00:25:54.020 --> 00:25:56.300
were now able to do
some of those things

00:25:56.300 --> 00:25:59.210
on our mobile devices.

00:25:59.210 --> 00:26:02.390
We also started, this
was the days of BYOD,

00:26:02.390 --> 00:26:03.890
we're still in those
days of course,

00:26:03.890 --> 00:26:06.140
but it was about
bringing your own device

00:26:06.140 --> 00:26:08.060
and not necessarily
the corporate liable

00:26:08.060 --> 00:26:09.080
locked down devices.

00:26:09.080 --> 00:26:12.470
But how do we allow
you as an employee

00:26:12.470 --> 00:26:15.420
to use your own device
for work purposes as well?

00:26:15.420 --> 00:26:19.350
But now where we are
is in mobility 3.0,

00:26:19.350 --> 00:26:22.100
which I really see as our
transformational phase.

00:26:22.100 --> 00:26:24.350
Now we're actually
trying to transform

00:26:24.350 --> 00:26:25.620
the way that we do work.

00:26:25.620 --> 00:26:27.161
So it's not just
taking what you used

00:26:27.161 --> 00:26:28.910
to do again on your
laptop or desktop

00:26:28.910 --> 00:26:31.070
and put it on a smaller
device, but now we're

00:26:31.070 --> 00:26:33.710
actually trying to change
the way that we actually

00:26:33.710 --> 00:26:35.139
do a business function.

00:26:35.139 --> 00:26:36.680
And we've got some
examples, and I'll

00:26:36.680 --> 00:26:38.060
show you some things of
what we're doing there.

00:26:38.060 --> 00:26:40.489
But this is really where
the cloud story comes in,

00:26:40.489 --> 00:26:41.780
which Andrew was talking about.

00:26:41.780 --> 00:26:44.270
Because a lot of these
capabilities that we need,

00:26:44.270 --> 00:26:47.780
all the personalization,
the contextual awareness,

00:26:47.780 --> 00:26:51.110
all of that is using
the cloud to help us

00:26:51.110 --> 00:26:55.050
with those types of solutions.

00:26:55.050 --> 00:26:57.770
So I wanted to just kind of
talk a little bit about what's

00:26:57.770 --> 00:27:03.230
happening in the area
of mobility again.

00:27:03.230 --> 00:27:06.260
Some would some would
say that, well, I

00:27:06.260 --> 00:27:07.970
think enterprise
mobility is actually

00:27:07.970 --> 00:27:10.430
becoming less about
mobility and more

00:27:10.430 --> 00:27:15.170
about these holistic
anywhere, anyhow, experiences.

00:27:15.170 --> 00:27:18.830
And so some people actually
say that the term mobile apps

00:27:18.830 --> 00:27:22.280
is now an outdated one
because maybe now they're

00:27:22.280 --> 00:27:24.657
digital apps, or maybe
their digital experiences,

00:27:24.657 --> 00:27:26.990
or maybe they're just apps,
whatever we want to call it.

00:27:26.990 --> 00:27:29.240
But we're moving away
from mobile apps.

00:27:29.240 --> 00:27:29.970
And why is that?

00:27:29.970 --> 00:27:33.770
It's because I think the mobile
device itself is changing.

00:27:33.770 --> 00:27:37.400
Typically, or traditionally
we've thought of mobile devices

00:27:37.400 --> 00:27:39.790
as being phones
or tablets, right?

00:27:39.790 --> 00:27:42.200
That's what we typically
classified it as.

00:27:42.200 --> 00:27:44.690
But now, we've got
wearables, whether it's

00:27:44.690 --> 00:27:46.670
your watches or
your smart glasses

00:27:46.670 --> 00:27:51.800
or whatever it may be, your
car is now mobile, it's running

00:27:51.800 --> 00:27:54.410
Android, it's got all
this capability in there,

00:27:54.410 --> 00:27:57.560
refrigerators, toothbrushes,
I mean all sorts of devices.

00:27:57.560 --> 00:28:00.140
Everything is now
a mobile device

00:28:00.140 --> 00:28:01.980
and we're treating it that way.

00:28:01.980 --> 00:28:04.070
So the device has
definitely changed.

00:28:04.070 --> 00:28:07.850
The way we're interacting,
as well, is also changing.

00:28:07.850 --> 00:28:10.280
Typically when you have
a mobile application,

00:28:10.280 --> 00:28:12.440
you're tapping on it,
you're selecting drop downs,

00:28:12.440 --> 00:28:14.240
you're entering
some text and forms,

00:28:14.240 --> 00:28:16.310
I mean it's that kind
of an application.

00:28:16.310 --> 00:28:20.870
But now we're using chat, we're
using messaging communications,

00:28:20.870 --> 00:28:22.880
we're using voice
more now actually,

00:28:22.880 --> 00:28:24.860
we're speaking
into the phone now

00:28:24.860 --> 00:28:27.920
for some of our
applications, Instant Apps.

00:28:27.920 --> 00:28:30.230
We've got Android
Instant Apps as well,

00:28:30.230 --> 00:28:33.310
that are changing the way
that we're interacting.

00:28:33.310 --> 00:28:35.840
So that is all changing.

00:28:35.840 --> 00:28:38.420
And then we've got these
new intermediaries.

00:28:38.420 --> 00:28:42.470
So ways that we're now
connecting and talking

00:28:42.470 --> 00:28:44.150
to our employees
and our customers

00:28:44.150 --> 00:28:46.700
is changing in ways that
we didn't have before.

00:28:46.700 --> 00:28:49.430
If I look at all the
transactions that actually

00:28:49.430 --> 00:28:51.950
happen through WhatsApp, or
all the transactions that

00:28:51.950 --> 00:28:54.140
are happening through
Facebook Messenger,

00:28:54.140 --> 00:28:57.380
or now with Google Home,
or Google Assistant,

00:28:57.380 --> 00:29:00.080
think of how we're actually
connecting and talking

00:29:00.080 --> 00:29:03.410
to these devices to interact
with our back end systems.

00:29:03.410 --> 00:29:04.970
So this is something
that's really

00:29:04.970 --> 00:29:08.120
changing again the
mobile applications

00:29:08.120 --> 00:29:13.560
to now digital applications
and digital experiences.

00:29:13.560 --> 00:29:16.610
So there's a few trends
here and where we're going.

00:29:16.610 --> 00:29:18.950
I think we've already
talked about device

00:29:18.950 --> 00:29:20.580
and app usage will
continue to rise.

00:29:20.580 --> 00:29:23.690
I think this sat around
70% of our workers

00:29:23.690 --> 00:29:25.730
today are already
mobile workers.

00:29:25.730 --> 00:29:27.740
That number will keep
growing, especially

00:29:27.740 --> 00:29:30.500
as we go to the
rest of the world.

00:29:30.500 --> 00:29:34.050
And then the apps, the app
usage, it also is increasing.

00:29:34.050 --> 00:29:36.509
So companies are building
more applications.

00:29:36.509 --> 00:29:38.550
And not only are they
building more applications,

00:29:38.550 --> 00:29:41.180
but they're providing
access to those applications

00:29:41.180 --> 00:29:43.562
to more and more users
within the organization.

00:29:43.562 --> 00:29:45.020
If you think about
before, maybe it

00:29:45.020 --> 00:29:47.660
were like pockets of
employees within a company

00:29:47.660 --> 00:29:50.214
that were actually using
mobile apps and mobile devices.

00:29:50.214 --> 00:29:51.380
But now we're opening it up.

00:29:51.380 --> 00:29:53.420
We're looking at
HR applications,

00:29:53.420 --> 00:29:55.800
finance applications,
marketing applications,

00:29:55.800 --> 00:29:57.350
and so on and so on.

00:29:57.350 --> 00:30:00.110
Beyond what we
traditionally thought of.

00:30:00.110 --> 00:30:01.880
Enterprises are going
to keep increasing

00:30:01.880 --> 00:30:04.520
the spending when it comes
to mobile applications

00:30:04.520 --> 00:30:05.720
and mobile devices.

00:30:05.720 --> 00:30:07.970
As more and more users
are starting to use it,

00:30:07.970 --> 00:30:10.100
they need more applications
or new applications,

00:30:10.100 --> 00:30:14.060
and the ability to provide
those solutions to those users

00:30:14.060 --> 00:30:17.000
so they will continue to
invest for the next few years

00:30:17.000 --> 00:30:19.070
in this space as well.

00:30:19.070 --> 00:30:21.110
I think now, like I
mentioned, I think

00:30:21.110 --> 00:30:23.750
we're seeing a need
for innovation.

00:30:23.750 --> 00:30:27.620
There has to be innovation
in all that our companies do.

00:30:27.620 --> 00:30:30.710
We, at Accenture say that every
business is a digital business,

00:30:30.710 --> 00:30:34.640
and you know whoops,
I went too far.

00:30:34.640 --> 00:30:37.550
But companies need to innovate.

00:30:37.550 --> 00:30:40.460
Whether you're a company that
is leading in your industry,

00:30:40.460 --> 00:30:43.310
you need to innovate to kind of
keep ahead of your competition.

00:30:43.310 --> 00:30:46.310
Or whether you're maybe a little
bit behind in your industry,

00:30:46.310 --> 00:30:49.760
but you need innovation
to stay alive, right?

00:30:49.760 --> 00:30:52.340
Because your business may
not exist if you don't keep

00:30:52.340 --> 00:30:54.140
innovating and keep
doing new things.

00:30:54.140 --> 00:30:57.620
So there is definitely
a need for innovation.

00:30:57.620 --> 00:30:58.829
Deep handset functionalities.

00:30:58.829 --> 00:31:01.078
When I saw this one, I thought
it made a lot of sense.

00:31:01.078 --> 00:31:03.620
Because if you think about
how we use mobile applications

00:31:03.620 --> 00:31:06.320
today, we typically
just use the screen

00:31:06.320 --> 00:31:08.710
when you think about
it for work purposes.

00:31:08.710 --> 00:31:12.110
You use the screen, you're maybe
inputting into an application,

00:31:12.110 --> 00:31:14.250
you're tapping here
and there or whatever.

00:31:14.250 --> 00:31:15.890
But we don't use the camera.

00:31:15.890 --> 00:31:18.410
We don't use all the sensors
that are on the device.

00:31:18.410 --> 00:31:19.940
I mean, our hardware
partners keep

00:31:19.940 --> 00:31:23.232
adding more and more
features into the devices,

00:31:23.232 --> 00:31:24.440
but we don't really use them.

00:31:24.440 --> 00:31:25.856
And I think that's
really starting

00:31:25.856 --> 00:31:28.400
to change now with some
of the new capabilities

00:31:28.400 --> 00:31:30.140
and new functions
that we have, which

00:31:30.140 --> 00:31:32.330
I'll show you some examples of.

00:31:32.330 --> 00:31:34.680
And then, as I said,
the mobile experiences

00:31:34.680 --> 00:31:36.350
would continue to
evolve as we have

00:31:36.350 --> 00:31:39.830
new ways of interacting
and new experiences

00:31:39.830 --> 00:31:42.140
that we're looking to
provide to our workers.

00:31:46.650 --> 00:31:51.300
So last fall, we announced
our partnership with Google.

00:31:51.300 --> 00:31:54.450
It really is all about
providing enterprise solutions

00:31:54.450 --> 00:31:57.820
to our customers,
to our clients,

00:31:57.820 --> 00:32:01.320
and it is all based on built
on top of the Google technology

00:32:01.320 --> 00:32:02.070
stack.

00:32:02.070 --> 00:32:03.900
You know, I started working
with Andrew and his team,

00:32:03.900 --> 00:32:05.790
and really looking at
how do we bring Android

00:32:05.790 --> 00:32:11.400
into the enterprise, and there
are definitely differentiators

00:32:11.400 --> 00:32:15.551
with Android when it comes to
the wide variety of devices

00:32:15.551 --> 00:32:16.050
out there.

00:32:16.050 --> 00:32:18.260
The form factors,
the price points.

00:32:18.260 --> 00:32:21.180
There's a number of
really strong reasons

00:32:21.180 --> 00:32:23.010
for picking Android.

00:32:23.010 --> 00:32:26.340
But one of the things we
said is that the real value

00:32:26.340 --> 00:32:29.220
is the entire Google
technology stack.

00:32:29.220 --> 00:32:30.870
There is so much
there that we can

00:32:30.870 --> 00:32:33.196
bring to life through
Android applications,

00:32:33.196 --> 00:32:35.820
and that's what we're trying to
show with some of the solutions

00:32:35.820 --> 00:32:37.860
and capabilities that we have.

00:32:37.860 --> 00:32:42.014
So I wanted to show you and talk
to you a little bit about what

00:32:42.014 --> 00:32:43.680
are some of these
innovations that we're

00:32:43.680 --> 00:32:45.300
seeing with our
clients, and what

00:32:45.300 --> 00:32:48.240
are some of the interesting
things that we're doing.

00:32:48.240 --> 00:32:49.710
And I'll hit a few of them.

00:32:49.710 --> 00:32:53.580
So collaboration is
one of those areas.

00:32:53.580 --> 00:32:55.230
You might not think
of collaboration

00:32:55.230 --> 00:32:56.550
as being so innovative.

00:32:56.550 --> 00:32:59.430
I mean we've been using
email, we've been using chat,

00:32:59.430 --> 00:33:02.370
we've been using all of
these things for some time.

00:33:02.370 --> 00:33:08.070
But what's not really there is
how do we actually incorporate

00:33:08.070 --> 00:33:09.990
that into our workflows?

00:33:09.990 --> 00:33:13.061
Like most of us, we're probably
on email all day, all the time,

00:33:13.061 --> 00:33:13.560
right?

00:33:13.560 --> 00:33:14.350
It's just there.

00:33:14.350 --> 00:33:16.600
It's one of the apps
we're constantly checking.

00:33:16.600 --> 00:33:18.510
But if you think of
like a store worker,

00:33:18.510 --> 00:33:20.490
they're not checking
email all the time.

00:33:20.490 --> 00:33:23.190
Or if you think of someone
on a manufacturing plant,

00:33:23.190 --> 00:33:24.906
they're not checking
email all the time.

00:33:24.906 --> 00:33:26.280
But that's a great
tool that they

00:33:26.280 --> 00:33:30.360
can use for various purposes.

00:33:30.360 --> 00:33:33.330
Even the integration with
calendar, the integration

00:33:33.330 --> 00:33:36.960
with Hangouts, or the
chat capabilities,

00:33:36.960 --> 00:33:38.640
being able to talk to people.

00:33:38.640 --> 00:33:41.340
I mean again, we may all be
on our computers all the time

00:33:41.340 --> 00:33:43.650
and just chatting with
people whenever we need to,

00:33:43.650 --> 00:33:46.860
but it's not so common in
the rest of the enterprise

00:33:46.860 --> 00:33:48.270
in the workforce.

00:33:48.270 --> 00:33:51.310
And the integration with
Drive as an example.

00:33:51.310 --> 00:33:54.810
So if I'm like a
salesperson, I don't

00:33:54.810 --> 00:33:58.110
want to go and search
somewhere for a document

00:33:58.110 --> 00:34:00.180
on a particular
product or something.

00:34:00.180 --> 00:34:03.229
I just want to be able to
go to my product catalog.

00:34:03.229 --> 00:34:04.770
I want to see what
that products are.

00:34:04.770 --> 00:34:06.870
And if I want more information
about that product,

00:34:06.870 --> 00:34:08.340
I want it to be right there.

00:34:08.340 --> 00:34:09.719
And so we can integrate that.

00:34:09.719 --> 00:34:11.520
We can integrate
all of these things

00:34:11.520 --> 00:34:15.120
into our mobile applications
to provide a better experience

00:34:15.120 --> 00:34:17.280
for workers, and something
that they're actually

00:34:17.280 --> 00:34:19.080
going to use day to day.

00:34:21.698 --> 00:34:22.489
Digital assistance.

00:34:22.489 --> 00:34:24.010
This is a really fun one.

00:34:24.010 --> 00:34:26.324
So have you heard of chat bots?

00:34:26.324 --> 00:34:28.929
Maybe some of you have
heard of chat bots?

00:34:28.929 --> 00:34:31.840
We're seeing a lot of really
cool examples of using

00:34:31.840 --> 00:34:34.900
chat bots on the consumer side,
as well as the enterprise side.

00:34:34.900 --> 00:34:39.040
So you know today, when
you're maybe booking a flight,

00:34:39.040 --> 00:34:42.100
or you're calling
customer service,

00:34:42.100 --> 00:34:44.741
you could use the chat
function that's there,

00:34:44.741 --> 00:34:46.449
like let's say on a
website or something,

00:34:46.449 --> 00:34:49.115
but that's usually a human being
on the other side of that who's

00:34:49.115 --> 00:34:50.493
actually responding to you.

00:34:50.493 --> 00:34:51.909
But now we're
getting to the point

00:34:51.909 --> 00:34:53.739
where it doesn't have
to be a human being.

00:34:53.739 --> 00:34:54.730
It's just a bot.

00:34:54.730 --> 00:34:58.480
It's something that,
it's a machine that's

00:34:58.480 --> 00:34:59.770
responding to you.

00:34:59.770 --> 00:35:02.440
And we've done a few
different examples

00:35:02.440 --> 00:35:05.170
in rollouts of chat
bots for a call center

00:35:05.170 --> 00:35:06.830
type of applications.

00:35:06.830 --> 00:35:08.980
But what we've actually
built out as well,

00:35:08.980 --> 00:35:13.060
are a chat bot for
workers, for employees.

00:35:13.060 --> 00:35:16.320
So we've got one as an
example for a field worker.

00:35:16.320 --> 00:35:18.520
So a field technician
who might go out

00:35:18.520 --> 00:35:21.880
in the field to fix something
or to work on something.

00:35:21.880 --> 00:35:24.790
Well maybe they might
come to a situation

00:35:24.790 --> 00:35:27.400
that they don't
know how to solve.

00:35:27.400 --> 00:35:29.755
And the worst case, as you
may know in field service

00:35:29.755 --> 00:35:31.930
is, to have to say you know
what, I can't fix this.

00:35:31.930 --> 00:35:33.763
I'm going to have to
send somebody else out.

00:35:33.763 --> 00:35:37.540
That's like the worst
situation that a field service

00:35:37.540 --> 00:35:39.070
company would want to be in.

00:35:39.070 --> 00:35:42.630
So we want to make these workers
productive, and self-sufficient

00:35:42.630 --> 00:35:43.630
while they're out there.

00:35:43.630 --> 00:35:45.880
So we gave them
the ability to just

00:35:45.880 --> 00:35:49.250
say, hey Google, why is this
transformer not working?

00:35:49.250 --> 00:35:50.950
And then it's using
machine learning

00:35:50.950 --> 00:35:53.710
to suggest things for you
to try to troubleshoot

00:35:53.710 --> 00:35:56.260
that particular item.

00:35:56.260 --> 00:35:58.180
So that's a great
example of how we

00:35:58.180 --> 00:36:00.700
can use chat bots for workers.

00:36:00.700 --> 00:36:02.870
We have another one
for a sales worker,

00:36:02.870 --> 00:36:04.870
where this one is a little
bit more intelligent.

00:36:04.870 --> 00:36:07.300
This one is more like
your personal assistant.

00:36:07.300 --> 00:36:09.670
So whereas a field
worker was more of a hey,

00:36:09.670 --> 00:36:13.870
help me troubleshoot this, the
sales worker application chat

00:36:13.870 --> 00:36:17.320
bot, which we called
Donna, Donna, is--

00:36:17.320 --> 00:36:19.750
you can just imagine
you, as a salesperson,

00:36:19.750 --> 00:36:22.780
are going to your client, "Hey
Donna, tell me about my day."

00:36:22.780 --> 00:36:25.850
And she'll actually speak
out and say, "You've

00:36:25.850 --> 00:36:26.900
got four meetings today.

00:36:26.900 --> 00:36:30.040
Your first meeting is
with blah blah blah."

00:36:30.040 --> 00:36:32.020
"Hey Donna, tell me
who are my hot leads?"

00:36:32.020 --> 00:36:34.420
And she can tell you these
are your hot leads in the area

00:36:34.420 --> 00:36:36.410
that you're going to today.

00:36:36.410 --> 00:36:39.760
Then she can get even smarter
and say, I can ask her,

00:36:39.760 --> 00:36:42.670
"Hey Donna, what products should
I recommend to this customer

00:36:42.670 --> 00:36:43.480
today?"

00:36:43.480 --> 00:36:46.164
And she can actually take
a look at all the things

00:36:46.164 --> 00:36:47.830
that are happening
around this customer.

00:36:47.830 --> 00:36:51.220
She can take a look at what
their past purchase history has

00:36:51.220 --> 00:36:54.580
been, what their
inventory levels might be,

00:36:54.580 --> 00:36:57.520
what their contract statuses
might be, and actually

00:36:57.520 --> 00:37:00.340
suggest things for
you, as a salesperson,

00:37:00.340 --> 00:37:02.140
to go and talk to
that client about.

00:37:02.140 --> 00:37:04.330
So it's about, again
using machine learning

00:37:04.330 --> 00:37:06.560
to make these
personal assistants,

00:37:06.560 --> 00:37:11.720
or chat bots there
as your helper.

00:37:14.520 --> 00:37:15.900
Image recognition.

00:37:15.900 --> 00:37:17.290
So this is a fun one.

00:37:17.290 --> 00:37:19.507
I'll actually show you
a demo of this one.

00:37:19.507 --> 00:37:21.090
Today, this morning
there was actually

00:37:21.090 --> 00:37:24.020
a press release about the work
that we just did with Rentokil.

00:37:24.020 --> 00:37:26.280
Rentokil is a pest
control company,

00:37:26.280 --> 00:37:29.070
and what they wanted
to do was, they

00:37:29.070 --> 00:37:32.520
wanted to equip their field
technicians with the ability

00:37:32.520 --> 00:37:36.450
to take a picture of insects,
cockroaches, beetles,

00:37:36.450 --> 00:37:39.870
whatever it is, and be able
to automatically identify

00:37:39.870 --> 00:37:41.430
what it is.

00:37:41.430 --> 00:37:42.930
Their technicians
are great, they're

00:37:42.930 --> 00:37:45.870
used to all sorts of bugs,
but they come across bugs

00:37:45.870 --> 00:37:48.000
that they don't necessarily
know or sometimes we'll

00:37:48.000 --> 00:37:49.030
get it wrong.

00:37:49.030 --> 00:37:50.970
And that could have a
big impact because it's

00:37:50.970 --> 00:37:53.460
really important for them
to know what type of bug

00:37:53.460 --> 00:37:56.130
it is so that they can provide
the right chemical treatment

00:37:56.130 --> 00:37:58.350
and chemical mixes to treat it.

00:37:58.350 --> 00:38:00.070
So that's what we did.

00:38:00.070 --> 00:38:03.120
So we actually
built an application

00:38:03.120 --> 00:38:06.210
that allows them to take
a picture of the bug

00:38:06.210 --> 00:38:07.360
and it will identify it.

00:38:07.360 --> 00:38:13.230
So if I can switch here,
I'll actually show you.

00:38:13.230 --> 00:38:15.570
I don't have any live
cockroaches here with me,

00:38:15.570 --> 00:38:17.946
but I do have a
couple of pictures

00:38:17.946 --> 00:38:19.320
and I'll show you
how that works.

00:38:23.910 --> 00:38:25.650
Can I cast it?

00:38:25.650 --> 00:38:27.790
There we go.

00:38:27.790 --> 00:38:30.060
So this is the application.

00:38:30.060 --> 00:38:33.170
So I'm just going to
come and identify a pest.

00:38:33.170 --> 00:38:37.590
So you can see here I've got
this lovely cockroach here,

00:38:37.590 --> 00:38:40.920
and I will take a picture
of this cockroach,

00:38:40.920 --> 00:38:42.186
and I will use it.

00:38:42.186 --> 00:38:43.560
And now again,
it's using machine

00:38:43.560 --> 00:38:47.010
learning to calculate and figure
out which type of bug this is.

00:38:47.010 --> 00:38:50.850
And we've trained it on a
number of different cockroaches,

00:38:50.850 --> 00:38:52.800
and beetles, and
so on, and so on.

00:38:52.800 --> 00:38:55.980
And it identified that this
is the American cockroach,

00:38:55.980 --> 00:38:58.590
and that's what my
cheat sheet says here.

00:38:58.590 --> 00:39:01.170
It is the American cockroach
that I actually just scanned.

00:39:01.170 --> 00:39:04.290
So it allows you to do that,
it allows the technician

00:39:04.290 --> 00:39:06.030
to see that picture
and actually compare,

00:39:06.030 --> 00:39:08.440
and say yup, that's
the right one,

00:39:08.440 --> 00:39:10.740
and it will provide them
some detail with it.

00:39:10.740 --> 00:39:13.850
All right, we go ahead
and select the match,

00:39:13.850 --> 00:39:16.220
then it's going to
populate the information

00:39:16.220 --> 00:39:19.040
in the application about
that particular bug

00:39:19.040 --> 00:39:20.780
or that treatment.

00:39:20.780 --> 00:39:22.250
Today they spend
a lot of time just

00:39:22.250 --> 00:39:24.410
manually going in and
having to fill this all out,

00:39:24.410 --> 00:39:26.540
now we've just automated it.

00:39:26.540 --> 00:39:31.250
And if I keep going then
it's going to provide me

00:39:31.250 --> 00:39:32.210
with a session summary.

00:39:32.210 --> 00:39:35.600
And what it does is, this
place was a pretty nasty place

00:39:35.600 --> 00:39:38.480
I guess, pretty infested
with all sorts of things.

00:39:38.480 --> 00:39:42.120
But now I can go and
view a remediation plan.

00:39:42.120 --> 00:39:45.320
So because it identified that
this is the American cockroach,

00:39:45.320 --> 00:39:48.230
it will tell me how to treat
it, what I should be doing,

00:39:48.230 --> 00:39:51.470
and actually will also provide
recommendations for a customer

00:39:51.470 --> 00:39:54.880
so that they can help to prevent
this from happening again

00:39:54.880 --> 00:39:56.730
in the future.

00:39:56.730 --> 00:39:58.370
So that's an
example of how we're

00:39:58.370 --> 00:40:02.409
using machine learning for
pest control, which I never

00:40:02.409 --> 00:40:03.200
thought I would do.

00:40:03.200 --> 00:40:06.170
But yes, that's an example.

00:40:06.170 --> 00:40:09.170
We can go back to
the screen please.

00:40:09.170 --> 00:40:13.040
Another example of using machine
learning for image recognition

00:40:13.040 --> 00:40:16.080
is what we did with our
smart parking application.

00:40:16.080 --> 00:40:19.550
So we've got this implemented
in a few different places today.

00:40:19.550 --> 00:40:21.860
But if you think about
your experience when you go

00:40:21.860 --> 00:40:24.470
and park in a garage, for
example, parking garage

00:40:24.470 --> 00:40:26.630
or a parking lot,
you might go and it

00:40:26.630 --> 00:40:30.020
might say that there
are 50 available spots.

00:40:30.020 --> 00:40:34.730
And it's calculating that based
on sensors that are actually

00:40:34.730 --> 00:40:37.580
in every single parking spot.

00:40:37.580 --> 00:40:40.310
That can be very expensive
to first of all implement,

00:40:40.310 --> 00:40:43.950
but also to maintain
in an ongoing manner.

00:40:43.950 --> 00:40:47.750
So what we did was we
actually trained the models

00:40:47.750 --> 00:40:52.490
to identify empty spots
versus occupied spots.

00:40:52.490 --> 00:40:54.830
So this is using
cameras, camera imagery.

00:40:54.830 --> 00:40:57.560
Many, many parking garage
and parking lots already

00:40:57.560 --> 00:41:00.050
have CCTV cameras
and other cameras

00:41:00.050 --> 00:41:02.990
already in place like that, so
we can pull from those images

00:41:02.990 --> 00:41:07.940
and just be able to detect
empty spot, occupied spot.

00:41:07.940 --> 00:41:10.730
Somebody's perhaps parked in
illegal parking spot where

00:41:10.730 --> 00:41:13.310
they shouldn't be parking,
and those notifications

00:41:13.310 --> 00:41:15.860
can go to the right
people to deal with that.

00:41:15.860 --> 00:41:21.990
But now, as a consumer,
or as an employee perhaps

00:41:21.990 --> 00:41:24.740
is looking in coming
to work in the morning,

00:41:24.740 --> 00:41:28.760
as I come into the parking
area, it can send me on my app,

00:41:28.760 --> 00:41:30.830
hey, your parking spot is here.

00:41:30.830 --> 00:41:33.770
Because you can now
reserve a parking spot.

00:41:33.770 --> 00:41:36.500
You go park in there
and it just allows you

00:41:36.500 --> 00:41:38.990
to direct the parking that way.

00:41:38.990 --> 00:41:41.540
So a really interesting use
case of how, again, we can use

00:41:41.540 --> 00:41:44.120
machine learning for parking.

00:41:47.250 --> 00:41:52.650
OK, so the last example I have
here is augmented reality.

00:41:52.650 --> 00:41:54.180
This is a really fun one.

00:41:54.180 --> 00:41:55.987
I think this is
where Google really

00:41:55.987 --> 00:41:58.320
shines because they've got
some cool technologies, which

00:41:58.320 --> 00:42:02.500
I don't think any
other companies do.

00:42:02.500 --> 00:42:08.094
So one example here
is the far left there.

00:42:08.094 --> 00:42:09.510
This was again for
a field worker.

00:42:09.510 --> 00:42:12.960
So the idea is that, as a
field worker, if I'm going out

00:42:12.960 --> 00:42:14.610
and I come across
an object again,

00:42:14.610 --> 00:42:17.010
I'm maybe not familiar
with it, or maybe it's new,

00:42:17.010 --> 00:42:19.140
or maybe I need to
learn how to do this,

00:42:19.140 --> 00:42:21.120
I can either use
my smart glasses

00:42:21.120 --> 00:42:23.670
or I can even use a phone,
and I can use the phone

00:42:23.670 --> 00:42:26.700
and just kind of hold
it up to that object,

00:42:26.700 --> 00:42:29.590
so this is a water pump, so I
can hold it up to the object,

00:42:29.590 --> 00:42:31.890
and it will provide
some overlays to me

00:42:31.890 --> 00:42:34.290
and tell me check this
transmitter level,

00:42:34.290 --> 00:42:35.220
check this valve.

00:42:35.220 --> 00:42:37.311
It will guide me,
and show arrows,

00:42:37.311 --> 00:42:39.060
and point to where I
should be looking at,

00:42:39.060 --> 00:42:41.940
and help me to troubleshoot
that particular water pump,

00:42:41.940 --> 00:42:43.669
as an example.

00:42:43.669 --> 00:42:44.460
We did another one.

00:42:44.460 --> 00:42:47.100
This screenshot here
is with the Tango,

00:42:47.100 --> 00:42:49.800
if you're familiar
with our Project Tango.

00:42:49.800 --> 00:42:54.490
This one we-- these screen shots
are from what we did with BMW.

00:42:54.490 --> 00:42:58.140
And they are now actually
rolling this application out

00:42:58.140 --> 00:43:00.180
to their showrooms
and allowing people

00:43:00.180 --> 00:43:06.222
to come in and virtualize a BMW
car in their environment, which

00:43:06.222 --> 00:43:06.930
is a really cool.

00:43:06.930 --> 00:43:08.263
And I can show you that as well.

00:43:08.263 --> 00:43:11.070
But what I wanted to show you
was an enterprise use case

00:43:11.070 --> 00:43:12.570
of using Project Tango.

00:43:12.570 --> 00:43:14.670
So if you're not
familiar, Project Tango

00:43:14.670 --> 00:43:18.260
is more of an augmented
reality type of scenario.

00:43:18.260 --> 00:43:21.900
We've also got some virtual
reality demos and solutions

00:43:21.900 --> 00:43:22.747
as well.

00:43:22.747 --> 00:43:25.330
Virtual reality is more when you
know you've got your head set

00:43:25.330 --> 00:43:28.270
and you're everything you're
seeing is in your virtual world

00:43:28.270 --> 00:43:28.770
there.

00:43:28.770 --> 00:43:31.950
But augmented reality is
actually using your real world

00:43:31.950 --> 00:43:36.090
and applying some additional
information or virtual objects

00:43:36.090 --> 00:43:38.970
within that, your real world.

00:43:38.970 --> 00:43:40.950
So think of this scenario.

00:43:40.950 --> 00:43:43.950
As a salesperson, I
go out to my client,

00:43:43.950 --> 00:43:46.180
and I go talk to him
about the products

00:43:46.180 --> 00:43:47.460
that I'm trying to sell.

00:43:47.460 --> 00:43:50.100
Well typically, I would
bring those products with me.

00:43:50.100 --> 00:43:54.060
Well, what happens if I'm in
a industry where the products

00:43:54.060 --> 00:43:57.810
that we make are too big,
and they're just too heavy,

00:43:57.810 --> 00:44:02.110
and I cannot physically bring
them with me to the meeting?

00:44:02.110 --> 00:44:04.350
So now that's where
we've got to this example

00:44:04.350 --> 00:44:07.080
here on Project Tango.

00:44:07.080 --> 00:44:12.520
So if I can head back to
the Chromecast, please.

00:44:12.520 --> 00:44:13.804
I need to switch devices.

00:44:23.440 --> 00:44:23.940
All right.

00:44:23.940 --> 00:44:26.400
So I'm just going
to load this app up.

00:44:26.400 --> 00:44:30.510
So simple name, augmented
reality catalog, ARCAT.

00:44:30.510 --> 00:44:32.250
Now what this is
going to do is, it's

00:44:32.250 --> 00:44:37.770
going to allow me to
virtualize an actual object

00:44:37.770 --> 00:44:39.750
in my environment.

00:44:39.750 --> 00:44:44.350
So if you look here, there's
no big drills or anything.

00:44:44.350 --> 00:44:48.090
So what I can do, is I
can select this object,

00:44:48.090 --> 00:44:52.970
and I'm going to
drop it right here.

00:44:52.970 --> 00:44:54.340
So now you see this object.

00:44:54.340 --> 00:44:57.060
This is the actual
size of the object.

00:44:57.060 --> 00:45:00.210
I can go around it, I can
take a closer look at it,

00:45:00.210 --> 00:45:03.440
I can even go inside
it, if I wanted to,

00:45:03.440 --> 00:45:07.950
and actually see what's going
on in that particular drill bit.

00:45:07.950 --> 00:45:11.820
And then I can
manipulate it, I can

00:45:11.820 --> 00:45:13.830
get some additional
information about it.

00:45:13.830 --> 00:45:17.730
So it's a great example of how
I can bring this object here.

00:45:17.730 --> 00:45:23.820
Now imagine if it's something
bigger like this big bit.

00:45:23.820 --> 00:45:25.710
This is the actual
size of this thing.

00:45:25.710 --> 00:45:28.080
So I've actually put it
here in my environment.

00:45:28.080 --> 00:45:30.900
Again, I can walk around it.

00:45:30.900 --> 00:45:35.040
I can take a look at all the
different pieces of this thing.

00:45:35.040 --> 00:45:39.120
I can see what this, get
as close as I want to

00:45:39.120 --> 00:45:39.750
for this thing.

00:45:39.750 --> 00:45:42.130
I can even go
inside, if I wanted

00:45:42.130 --> 00:45:47.200
to see what's in it for
those types of use cases.

00:45:47.200 --> 00:45:51.150
So again, a great
example of how I can

00:45:51.150 --> 00:45:52.720
bring this technology
to my client.

00:45:52.720 --> 00:45:56.070
But now what if I want to
show how it actually works?

00:45:56.070 --> 00:46:00.610
So I can do simulations,
if I can place it here.

00:46:00.610 --> 00:46:01.880
OK, so this is the drill bit.

00:46:01.880 --> 00:46:05.290
And it's actually showing me how
it works because it's actually

00:46:05.290 --> 00:46:08.870
drilling into the ground.

00:46:08.870 --> 00:46:11.696
Now this device, it recognizes
and understands the space

00:46:11.696 --> 00:46:12.320
that you're in.

00:46:12.320 --> 00:46:14.670
So it can figure out
that this is the floor,

00:46:14.670 --> 00:46:17.990
those are the walls, that's
the ceiling up there.

00:46:17.990 --> 00:46:22.030
But this is the actual
size of this drill bit.

00:46:22.030 --> 00:46:25.722
And you can see, if I can
bring this to my customers,

00:46:25.722 --> 00:46:27.430
it will really show
them how it all works

00:46:27.430 --> 00:46:32.960
and what the actual
experience is like.

00:46:32.960 --> 00:46:35.710
So if we can go back
to the screen, please.

00:46:38.940 --> 00:46:41.040
So those are some
examples of innovation

00:46:41.040 --> 00:46:43.470
and what we're actually
doing with customers today.

00:46:43.470 --> 00:46:45.810
And at this point I'll
invite Andrew back up,

00:46:45.810 --> 00:46:49.440
and take it from there.

00:46:49.440 --> 00:46:54.495
